/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  borate01_felmyWeare --- boron in the Pitzer-HMW model, every parameter read
  from its primary: Felmy & Weare, Geochim. Cosmochim. Acta 50 (1986) 2771-2783.

  WHAT THE CASE ANSWERS.  Boric acid is a weak acid that is also a neutral
  molecule, an orthoborate ion, two polyborate ions and (in seawater) two ion
  pairs with calcium and magnesium.  Can the engine's Pitzer model, loaded with
  the paper's tables and nothing else, reproduce the paper's own species
  distribution, and does it then land on Dickson's apparent constant of boric
  acid in seawater?  Two operations, both `speciate` at 25 C:

   1. borax_table1 -- water saturated with borax (Na2B4O7.10H2O).  Totals: Na
      0.323 and boron 0.646 mol/kg (Na : B = 1 : 2, the borax stoichiometry,
      `totalsBasis stoichiometric`); the pH is SOLVED.  Network: water
      dissociation, B(OH)4-, B3O3(OH)4-, B4O5(OH)4 2-.
      Table 1 (p. 2772) prints the answer:  B(OH)3 .081, B(OH)4- .131,
      B3O3(OH)4- .050, B4O5(OH)4 2- .071 mol/kg.
      Choupo:  .0794, .1311, .0517, .0701 (pH 9.27).
   2. seawater_boron_pH82 -- the reference seawater of
      docs/design/boron-in-seawater-apparent-pka-study.md section 3 at the GIVEN
      pH 8.2 (Br folded into Cl, Sr into Ca, carbonate left out: the study says
      why), with the posture of the parameterisation: the major ions unpaired,
      CaB(OH)4+ and MgB(OH)4+ explicit.  Choupo: pK*F = 8.6765 against Dickson's
      8.6896 (free scale, molal): the borate fraction is 0.6 % low in free boric
      acid.

  WHAT IS HELD, AND HOW FAR IT CAN BE TRUSTED (read this before the numbers).
   * The ANCHOR rows of the `expected` file are the paper's Table 1 and the
     Dickson value above.  Table 1 is CALCULATED BY THE PAPER'S OWN MODEL, from
     the very parameters that were fitted to the data: it is IN-SAMPLE.  Its
     agreement shows that the tables were transcribed and the engine implements
     the same sums (neutral lambda and zeta terms, ion pairs, polyborate
     virials); it does not test the model against nature.
   * The Dickson row is PARTLY INDEPENDENT.  The seawater measurement is not in
     the paper's fit, but the Na, Ca and Mg borate terms were fitted to the emf
     data of the same laboratory family (Hershey et al. 1985; Dickson,
     unpublished, for K).  Dickson's pK*B itself is quoted from the study, not
     read from Dickson (1990), and moved to the free scale there.
   * The formation constants of B(OH)4-, the polyborates and the two ion pairs
     are the public-domain databank's (pitzer.dat); the paper prints the
     standard chemical potentials (Table 2) from which they follow with the
     chemical potentials of H2O, Ca2+ and Mg2+ of Harvie, Moller & Weare (1984),
     which the project does not hold.  The three that need only H2O (B(OH)4-,
     B3O3(OH)4-, B4O5(OH)4 2-) reproduce to 4 digits with the recalled H2O value
     (data/curation/batches/boron_c198/formation_check.tsv); the
     Ca pair agrees (log K -7.5887) with a recalled
     Ca2+ value; the Mg pair is carried unchecked.
   * 25 C only.  The paper gives no temperature dependence; the engine announces
     any run away from 298.15 K.
   * The case is not sealed: the records come from data/standards.  Not read
     here: the Searles Lake application (Tables 6-14, solids), which needs
     mineral records the engine does not carry for these salts.

  The pH scale is the HMW one (free H+ activity, single-ion convention); the
  seawater pH 8.2 is GIVEN on that scale.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    // -- 1. Borax-saturated water, 25 C: the species distribution of Table 1 (p. 2772).
    //    Totals are the stoichiometry of Na2B4O7: Na = 0.323 and
    //    boron = 0.081 + 0.131 + 3 x 0.050 + 4 x 0.071 = 0.646 mol/kg (Na : B = 1 : 2).
    //    The pH is not given: electroneutrality closes it.
    {
        name        borax_table1;
        type        speciate;
        temperature 298.15;
        analyticalTotals      { Na 0.323 mol/kg; H3BO3 0.646 mol/kg; }
        totalsBasis stoichiometric;   // what was dissolved (borax), not a measured analysis
        pH          solve;
        networkScope restricted;
        network      ( water-dissociation BOH4-formation B3O3OH4-formation B4O5OH4-formation );
        reason       "the borate network of the Felmy & Weare parameterisation: boric acid, the orthoborate and the two polyborates; no ion pairing of the major ions";
        diagSpecies ( Na H3BO3 BOH4 B3O3OH4 B4O5OH4 H );
        output      { file borax_table1.csv; }
    }

    // -- 2. Seawater boron at pH 8.2, 25 C (reference seawater of the study
    //    docs/design/boron-in-seawater-apparent-pka-study.md section 3, Br folded into Cl and Sr into Ca).
    {
        name        seawater_boron_pH82;
        type        speciate;
        temperature 298.15;
        analyticalTotals { Na 0.48606 mol/kg; Mg 0.0547421 mol/kg; Ca 0.0107508 mol/kg;
                           K 0.0105797 mol/kg; Cl 0.566638 mol/kg; SO4 0.0292643 mol/kg;
                           H3BO3 4.3116e-4 mol/kg; }
        pH          8.2;
        networkScope restricted;
        network      ( water-dissociation BOH4-formation CaBOH4-formation MgBOH4-formation );
        reason       "the posture of the parameterisation: the major ions unpaired, the calcium and magnesium borate ion pairs explicit";
        diagSpecies ( H H3BO3 BOH4 CaBOH4 MgBOH4 );
        output      { file seawater_boron_pH82.csv; }
    }
);
